# Chapter 15 — The Console Remembers

Everything you have built so far vanishes the moment the console turns off. The
hero, the score, the coins collected — gone. That is fine for a single sitting,
but a *real* game remembers. It knows your high score from yesterday. It knows
your name. It knows which level you reached before you had to stop for dinner.
This chapter gives your game a memory that outlives a single play.

The console keeps a little notebook. You write something into it under a name,
and later — even after the machine has been off — you can ask for that name and
get your thing back. Two verbs do the whole job: `cortex/remember` writes a fact,
and `cortex/recall` reads it. Everything about saving a game is those two.

## An empty notebook

Before you have told it anything, the console remembers nothing. Ask it to recall
a high score it has never been given, and it answers honestly:

```motoi
(display (cortex/recall 'high-score))
(newline)
```

```
#f
```

`#f` — *nothing here*. The console does not make up a score to be polite; it tells
you the truth, that it has never heard of `high-score`. This honest empty answer
matters: it is how your game knows a player is brand new. No saved score means
*first time playing* — show them the tutorial, start them at zero.

## Teach it a number

Now write something down. `cortex/remember` takes a *name* and a *value* and files
the value under that name. To read the value back cleanly, we use a tiny helper —
copy it as-is for now; it just digs the number out of the console's memory record:

```motoi
(define (high-score)
  (let ((r (cortex/recall 'high-score)))
    (if r (cadr (assq ':data r)) 0)))
(cortex/remember 'high-score 1000)
(display (high-score))
(newline)
```

```
1000
```

You told the console `remember high-score is 1000`, and when you asked for it
back, there it was: `1000`. That is a saved game in its smallest form. The name
`high-score` is the label on the drawer; `1000` is what is in the drawer; and the
`high-score` helper is just a hand that reaches in and pulls the number out. Write
once, read any time.

## Beat the record

A high score is only interesting when you *beat* it. Remembering the same name
again simply replaces what was there — the console keeps the newest fact, not a
pile of old ones:

```motoi
(define (high-score)
  (let ((r (cortex/recall 'high-score)))
    (if r (cadr (assq ':data r)) 0)))
(cortex/remember 'high-score 1000)
(cortex/remember 'high-score 2500)
(display (high-score))
(newline)
```

```
2500
```

You saved `1000`, then a better run saved `2500`, and the notebook now reads
`2500` — the old score is gone, replaced by the new best. This is the exact shape
of a high-score table: at the end of each game, if this run beat the saved score,
`remember` it; the next player sees the new number to chase. One name, always
holding the best.

## It remembers words too

Memory is not only for numbers. Store a string and the console keeps it just the
same — a player's name, the level they are on, the color they picked:

```motoi
(define (champion)
  (let ((r (cortex/recall 'champion)))
    (if r (cadr (assq ':text r)) \"nobody\")))
(cortex/remember 'champion \"Kit\")
(display (champion))
(newline)
```

```
Kit
```

You remembered that the champion is `\"Kit\"`, and asked back, the console said
`Kit`. Same two verbs, a word instead of a number. Between them, `cortex/remember`
and `cortex/recall` can hold everything a saved game needs: the high score, the
hero's name, the furthest level, the settings — each under its own name, each
waiting for the next time the console wakes up.

## What you just proved

You gave your game a memory. You asked an empty console for a score and got the
honest `#f`; you taught it `high-score` is `1000` and read it back; you beat that
with `2500` and watched the newest fact replace the old; and you stored the word
`\"Kit\"` under `champion` and recalled it too. Every program ran and the console
kept exactly what you told it.

Your game can now draw, move, respond, collide, sing, *and* remember. That is a
whole game — nothing is missing. All that is left is to wrap it up and hand it to
someone else, which is the last thing this book will teach: how to pack your game
into a cart and ship it.

> **Tip**
>
> Give every saved thing a clear, specific name: `'high-score`, `'player-name`,
> `'level-reached`, `'music-on`. The name is how you find it again, so vague names
> like `'x` or `'data` become a mystery a week later. And always handle the empty
> case — a `cortex/recall` that returns `#f` means *never saved*, and your game
> should have a sensible default ready (score `0`, name `\"nobody\"`, level `1`)
> for the very first play.

> **Warning**
>
> `cortex/remember` writes to the console's memory on disk, so what you save
> really does survive the machine being turned off — that is the whole point, but
> it also means a save *sticks*. If you remember a wrong or test value under a real
> name, it stays remembered until you overwrite it. When you are just experimenting,
> use a throwaway name like `'test-score` so you do not clobber a real save. The
> console remembers faithfully, including your mistakes. Appendix F covers the cart
> and where saved memory lives.

